course: biology
lesson: 96
title: The petting farm: goats, sheep, pigs, rabbits and washing hands
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: At the petting farm the goats rush to the fence the moment you hold out food, the pig flops into the mud, the cow licks your sleeve and the donkey shouts «hee-haw».
explain: Each of these animals is built for its own life: the goat's flat pupils watch the ground for wolves, the cow's long tongue grabs grass, and the rabbit's front teeth never stop growing.
explain: Many cartoon ideas about farm animals are wrong: goats do not eat tin cans, pigs are not dirty, donkeys are not stupidly stubborn, and Bugs Bunny's carrots are not good rabbit food.
explain: The animals look clean and healthy, but their fur and droppings can carry germs that upset people's tummies, so everyone washes with soap at the sinks by the exit.
fact: Goats are curious browsers that nibble a bit of everything, but they do not really eat tin cans; they nibble paper labels and test things with their lips.
fact: Goats and sheep have wide, flat pupils that give a wide view of the ground to spot predators, and when a goat lowers its head its eyes turn to keep the pupils level.
fact: Farm sheep grow wool all the time and must be shorn every year, because people bred them to keep their wool; a sheep called Shrek in New Zealand escaped shearing for six years and grew 27 kilograms of wool.
fact: Guinea pigs come from the Andes mountains of South America; they are prey animals and feel safest held low, close and gently, and they are neither pigs nor from Guinea; nobody is sure how they got the name.
fact: The front teeth, or incisors, of rabbits and guinea pigs grow all their lives and are worn down by chewing tough grass and hay.
fact: Too many carrots are sugary and bad for rabbits, whose food should be mostly hay and grass; Bugs Bunny chewing carrots all day is a cartoon idea.
fact: Pigs have very few sweat glands, so they roll in mud to cool down and protect their skin, and given space they keep a separate spot for their toilet.
fact: Pigs can learn tricks, solve puzzles and, in experiments, play simple video games with a joystick, and they find buried food with their noses.
fact: A cow wraps its long rough tongue round grass because it has no top front teeth, only a hard pad.
fact: A cow's stomach has four parts, and it chews its food twice, bringing it back up as cud.
fact: Chickens scratch the ground for seeds, worms and insects like their wild ancestor, the red junglefowl of Asian forests, and there are more chickens than any other bird.
fact: Farm animals can carry germs like E. coli on their fur and in their droppings without being ill, so soap and water are best, and you should not eat in the petting area.
fact: Donkeys bray loudly to keep in touch over long distances, as their wild ancestors in African deserts lived spread out, and they stop to think when unsure rather than being stubborn.
exercise: Do goats really eat tin cans? | No, they nibble labels and test things with their lips | Yes, they need the metal in the cans to grow strong horns | Yes, but only empty ones | No, they eat only grass
exercise: What shape are a goat's pupils? | Wide, long and flat | Round like ours | Tall upright slits | Star-shaped
exercise: Why do grazing animals have flat pupils? | To see a wide view of the ground and spot predators | To see colours better | To see far up into the sky and spot rain clouds coming | To see in the dark
exercise: Why must farm sheep be shorn every year? | People bred them to keep their wool instead of shedding it | Wool makes them ill in winter | Their wool slowly turns into thick hair if it is left uncut | Shearing makes the wool grow faster
exercise: How much wool did Shrek the runaway sheep grow in six years? | 27 kilograms | 2 kilograms | 270 kilograms | 500 grams
exercise: Where do guinea pigs first come from? | The Andes mountains of South America | Guinea in Africa | The old farms of southern England and Wales | The deserts of Australia
exercise: How should you hold a guinea pig? | Low, close and gently | High up over your head | By the scruff of its neck | Upside down on your arm
exercise: What is special about a rabbit's front teeth? | They grow all its life | They fall out every year | They are made of soft bone | They stop growing at one year old
exercise: What should most of a rabbit's food be? | Hay and grass | Carrots | Bread | Lettuce only
exercise: Why does a pig roll in mud on a hot day? | It has few sweat glands and the mud cools it | It just likes being dirty, muddy and smelly | It is looking for its food | It is cleaning its teeth
exercise: What have pigs learned to do in experiments? | Play simple video games with a joystick | Read short words aloud in a squeaky voice | Count to a hundred | Drive a toy car on roads
exercise: Why does a cow use its long tongue to grab grass? | It has no top front teeth | Its lips are far too small | Its teeth are too sharp | It cannot bend its neck
exercise: How many parts does a cow's stomach have? | Four | One | Two | Seven
exercise: What is cud? | Food a cow brings back up to chew a second time | The thick, creamy milk that a young calf drinks | The grass a cow sleeps on | The fluff on a cow's ear
exercise: Why does a chicken scratch the ground? | To uncover seeds, worms and insects | To dig a deep, cosy nest for its eggs | To sharpen its claws | To keep its feet warm
exercise: Why are there sinks at the petting farm exit? | Animals can carry germs like E. coli that make people ill | The animals are all very sick | To wash the strong animal smell off your coats and clothes | To give the animals water
exercise: Why does a donkey bray so loudly? | To call other donkeys far away | Because it is angry with people | Because it is in pain | To scare the goats
test: we fed 3 goats and 2 sheep, no hang on, 3 sheep. how many legs at the fence | 24 | 20 | 12 | 28
test: 4 cows in the barn, how many stomach parts altogether | 16 | 4 | 8 | 12
test: 2 chickens and a goat, how many legs is that | 8 | 12 | 6 | 10
test: In Bugs Bunny the rabbit munches carrots all day. What would a vet say? | Too many carrots are sugary; rabbits need mostly hay | Carrots are all a rabbit ever needs | Rabbits cannot eat carrots at all | Rabbits need a little meat every day as well as carrots
test: In Peppa Pig the family loves jumping in muddy puddles. Why do real pigs love mud? | It cools them and protects their skin | They want to look funny | They are dirty animals by nature | They are hunting for juicy worms in the mud
test: In a cartoon a goat chews up a whole tin can. What would you fix? | Goats nibble the paper label, not the metal | Nothing, goats digest metal | Goats only eat glass | Goats eat the cans but always spit out the paper label
test: What would happen to a rabbit that ate only soft food? | Its teeth would grow too long | It would grow very fast | Its fur would turn white | Nothing at all
test: What could happen to a farm sheep that is never shorn? | Its wool would grow huge and heavy | Its wool would fall off by itself in spring | Its wool would turn into hair | It would grow horns instead
test: just ate my crisps in the goat pen, its fine right? | No, wash hands with soap first and eat away from the animals | Yes, goat germs can never hurt people, only other goats | Yes, if you use hand gel only | Yes, the goats will eat the crumbs
test: Your uncle says donkeys are stupid and stubborn. What is fairer? | They stop to think when unsure, a careful habit | They are stubborn because they cannot hear you well | Yes, they are the least clever farm animal | They refuse to move because they are lazy
test: Why are guinea pigs called guinea pigs? | Nobody is sure, as they are neither pigs nor from Guinea | They come from Guinea in Africa and are a kind of small pig | They were sold for one guinea coin as pigs | They oink like pigs in Guinea
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the pig is observed to wallow in mud in warm weather. | It possesses few sweat glands and mud serves to cool the skin | It derives its food principally from mud | It is by nature disposed towards uncleanliness and enjoys dirt | It thereby removes parasites from its teeth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the incisors of the rabbit? | They continue to grow throughout life and are worn by tough fodder | They are shed each spring and are replaced by a fresh new set annually | They cease growing at maturity | They are composed of soft cartilage
exam: It is given that three cows and two goats are housed together. Ascertain the aggregate number of legs. | Twenty | Twelve | Ten | Sixteen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning visitors to a petting farm. | Hands ought to be washed with soap and water upon departure | Hand gel alone is invariably sufficient | Food may safely be consumed amongst the animals | Washing is required only if one of the animals appears to be unwell
